  "functions": [
    "FUNC.OP_RANGE_REF"
  ],
  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "Range operator (:) forms one normalized area, allows whitespace around :, and spans chained operands",
  "statement": "In Excel 16.0.20527, the range operator (:) takes two references on one sheet and produces a single rectangular area (AREAS(A1:C7) = 1, ROWS(A:A) = 1048576, ISREF(A1:B2) = TRUE). Whitespace around the colon still parses as the range operator rather than space intersection (=SUM(A1 : A3), =SUM(A1 :A3), and =SUM(A1: A3) all return 6; INDIRECT(\"$A1 : B$2\") resolves, whereas INDIRECT(\"A1:B2:C3\") returns #REF!). Chained ranges in worksheet formulas span their bounding box (=ROWS(A1:B2:C4) = 4, Excel 16.0 on 2026-10-23).",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "Measured across Excel 16.0.20527 in hjd (rows 1-3), dc_51ka (rows 44, 46, 51), cfunc_m3_6 (reference_result row 48, lookup_edges row 320), dc_nzpw (row 92), and 2026-10-23 Excel 16.0 checks in refops.rs / l1.rs.",
  "inferred": null,
  "documentation": null,
